{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, which refers to the asymmetry of a probability distribution. A left-skewed lottery has a higher concentration of probability on more favorable outcomes. For Lottery A (500 Euros with 100% probability), the outcome is certain, so the distribution is not skewed—it is a single point. For Lottery B (1000 Euros with 50%, 0 Euros with 50%), the distribution is symmetric around its mean (500 Euros), meaning it is not left- or right-skewed. Therefore, neither lottery is clearly more left-skewed than the other. Lottery A could be considered more 'favorable' in terms of skewness only if one interprets certainty as maximal left-skewness (all probability on the best possible outcome, given no risk), but this is a debatable interpretation. Strictly speaking, skewness requires variation in outcomes to compute, so Lottery A has undefined skewness. Thus, the decision reason is only marginally applicable, and the comparison based on skewness does not clearly favor one lottery over the other.",
